in this video we're going to be using L  systems or Lindenmayer systems for  procedural plant generation some quick  background L systems or Lindenmayer  systems have been around for a long time  they were originally devised by our  estate Lyndon Mayer a Hungarian  theoretical biologist and botanist and  he was using them at least initially to  describe the development of simple  multicellular organisms later on the  system was extend to describe more  complex branching structures like plants  so that's interesting but what exactly  is an L system basically they're like a  set of symbols and rules and you  iteratively apply those rules over and  over again getting a larger set of  symbols which you then in turn convert  into geometric structure if that sounds  like just a pile of words let's go over  this bit by bit there are a few  definitions you need to start with the  alphabet which is the set of symbols  that we're using the axiom which is just  a fancy word for the set of starting  symbols or like the initial state and  then there are the rules which you apply  to create more simple so let's walk  through an example using our own symbols  we're gonna start with these two symbols  F and R which stand for forward and  right and forward in this example means  move forward a little bit draw a line  from the start and end point and right  means turn right in this example it  literally just means turn right and so  starting with the axiom F R and with the  rules that F turns into F are F so  starting with FR FR turns into f r FR  because f substitutes with Fr F and then  FR FR turns into FR FR FR FR and as you  can see as we drew along with this the  final example was a square so you might  be able to make out from this that  there's a recursive nature to l-systems  and that as you iterate and replace  parts of the string a lot of  self-similarity arises naturally when  you look at plants they have the same  recursive structure to them the trunk of  a tree branches off into branches which  in turn branch off into smaller branches  etc etc and you can see that self  and all sorts of plans where the smaller  bits look like shrunken versions of the  entire thing so now how to implement  this there's two major parts that you  need to implement first is the  construction of the sentence which is  the result of applying as many rules as  you can and the second part is given a  sentence  ie a set of symbols translating those  symbols into a geometric shape  in other words drawing it so let's  tackle the first part constructing the  sentence and you'll be pleased to know  that this is incredibly simple in the  code this is done through the function  apply rules and you can see from the  code here that all it does is it starts  with the axiom it uses this helper  function apply rules to sentence which  takes a sentence applies all the rules  and returns a new sentence looking in  apply rules to sentence what this does  is it starts by looping over every  character in the sentence and then for  that character it looks for rules it  applies to that character it uses this  helper here a fine matching rule to find  the rules it applies and if it does we  add those new symbols to the new  sentence otherwise we just put back the  old one now to draw this we look at the  render function what we do here is get  the canvas and clear it out then we set  an initial transform and this is just so  that when we draw all the operations  take place from the middle of the canvas  at the bottom of the screen because  these are plants we want them to grow up  so quick sidenote the symbols that we're  using in these examples now like F plus  + bracket whatever these look kind of  confusing but we use these for  historical reasons originally a lot of  these refer to the commands for the  turtle graphics library which dates back  to 1967 we don't have to do that anymore  and I ditch them in more complex  examples for now if we use them as is we  get to use the original examples that  were given by Lyndon Mayer so let's just  use them anyway back to the code so all  we do is iterate the list of symbols and  sequence the symbols we support for now  our F which means draw a line plus and  minus which just means turn a little bit  to the left or a little bit to the right  and the square brackets which are just a  way of managing the state of the  transform  we push or pop the state depending on  whether or not receive the opening or  closing bracket so as he loop over the  list of symbols and draw them we end up  getting tree-like structures in the UI  here I can play with the number of  iterations that is the number of times I  loop over the sentence applying the  rules and this really helps to see the  self similarity with the very first  preset in no iteration so you can see  the basic tree structure but if I drag  this over by wooden suddenly the  complexity of the tree jumps up a lot  but you can see that it's just the same  tree pasted again over and over again  just smaller take a look at this new  branch on the right and you can see that  it's just a smaller version of the tree  from the first iteration  that's that self similarity I'm talking  about I've also added a bunch of presets  most of these are taken straight from  the original book by Lyndon Mayer the  algorithmic beauty of plants which is  free online if you want to take a look  you can just find it on google or it's  in the description below  I also added leaves they're just drawn  right at the end of segments that don't  branch off and you can play with the  color or the shape as I'm here to make  them longer or fatter I've also added a  few other knobs and dials for you to  play with so you can fit around the  length and width of segments of the  plants there's also this angle parameter  when playing with this can change the  look for the plants pretty dramatically  so you can see that I can kind of make a  weed out of a tree just by making a  little more yellowish and tidying it up  by decreasing the angle you'll also  notice that I have a complex HTML and in  experimental HTML and we've been looking  at the basic HTML but if you're  interested let's take a look at the more  complicated versions in the more complex  version we implement some slightly more  advanced rules in this version we allow  some variation on all the parameters and  that's controlled by this variability  parameter up here which is in essence a  randomness parameter so as I increase  the randomness the angles the lengths  and sizes of things they can all get  increasingly random we've also added a  stochastic grammar meaning that it's  possible for more than one rule to match  at a time with a given probability and  that means that it adds a lot more  randomness to the growth since branches  may or may not appear depending  which version you get and as I change  the random seed lots of variations of  the same tree appear here and finally I  have an experimental version that adds  and tracks the age of every component so  you get a more natural growth animation  which just kind of looks neat anyway  that's as far as we're gonna go today  hopefully you enjoyed this and if you  want to see more content like it hit  subscribe and like the video  Cheers
 

